# SHAHAB-025 — ACOUSTIC FORCED ALIGNMENT, WORD-LEVEL TIMING, PHONETIC BOUNDARY ANALYSIS & TIMESTAMP INTEGRITY

## 0. MISSION OBJECTIVE

Engineer a reliable acoustic forced-alignment subsystem for SHAHAB.

The primary objective is to establish the actual temporal relationship between a supplied transcript and its corresponding source audio.

The system must determine, to the granularity genuinely supported by its alignment method:

- Where a transcript segment begins.
- Where it ends.
- Where each word begins and ends, when word-level alignment is supported.
- Which audio interval corresponds to a token or phonetic unit, where the selected method can provide that granularity.
- Which transcript regions cannot be aligned reliably.
- Which parts of the audio contain speech not represented in the supplied transcript.
- Which transcript content cannot be located in the audio.
- Which boundaries are uncertain or ambiguous.

This mission introduces an alignment capability, not another speech-recognition engine.

The alignment subsystem must not silently rewrite the supplied transcript.

The alignment subsystem must not pretend that text is acoustically confirmed merely because it can assign timestamps to the text.

The alignment subsystem must not fabricate evenly distributed word timestamps.

The alignment subsystem must not convert a weak alignment score into a confident correctness claim.

The alignment subsystem must preserve the exact relationship between:

- The audio source.
- The audio sample timeline.
- The supplied transcript revision.
- The tokenizer or phonetic representation.
- The alignment algorithm.
- The resulting word and segment boundaries.

The intended result is an independently testable SHAHAB service that can align recognized or user-provided Persian text to its corresponding audio while preserving uncertainty and provenance.

