## 5. PERSIAN TEXT PREPARATION FOR ALIGNMENT

Persian alignment requires special care because written forms and acoustic realizations are not always one-to-one.

Inspect the actual tokenizer or phonetic frontend required by the selected alignment model.

Do not use a generic Persian normalizer without evaluating its effect on acoustic correspondence.

### 5.1 Orthographic normalization

Preserve:

- Persian Yeh and Arabic Yeh distinctions.
- Persian Kaf and Arabic Kaf.
- ZWNJ.
- Word boundaries.
- Compound expressions.
- Colloquial spelling.
- Informal verb forms.
- Punctuation.
- Numerals.
- Mixed Persian/Latin tokens.
- Foreign words.
- Proper nouns.

If alignment preparation requires normalization, store:

- Original transcript.
- Alignment-prepared transcript.
- Transformation revision.
- Reversible mapping between representations.

Never overwrite the source text with alignment-normalized text.

### 5.2 Token-to-word mapping

A tokenizer may split one written Persian word into multiple subword tokens.

The alignment system must maintain a mapping between:

- Original orthographic word.
- Normalized alignment word.
- Subword tokens.
- Phonetic units, where available.
- Source sample interval.

Do not assume one token equals one word.

Do not combine subword timings by naïvely averaging them.

Define a boundary aggregation method that is compatible with the actual model.

### 5.3 Spoken versus written forms

A spoken contraction may differ from its written representation.

The aligner must not silently convert the spoken form into a formal equivalent and then present that equivalence as exact alignment.

Where a phonetic equivalence or alternative pronunciation is required, document the mapping and preserve the original transcript form.

### 5.4 Numbers and symbols

A written number may correspond to multiple spoken words, or a spoken phrase may be normalized into a numeric form.

Preserve the relationship between:

- Spoken lexical sequence.
- Written transcript form.
- Normalized numeric representation.

If the alignment model cannot map a normalized numeric token to the underlying spoken sequence, mark that region unresolved rather than inventing word boundaries.

### 5.5 Punctuation

Punctuation is not an acoustic token in the same sense as spoken words.

Do not assign a fabricated acoustic interval to punctuation.

Where punctuation is retained in the transcript, associate it with a neighboring word or sentence boundary using a documented non-acoustic representation.

## 6. ALIGNMENT METHOD RESEARCH AND SELECTION

Inspect suitable alignment approaches based on the actual model and available runtime.

Potential approaches may include:

- CTC-based forced alignment.
- Transducer-compatible alignment, where a valid method and compatible implementation exist.
- Model-specific attention alignment, only where reliable semantics are documented.
- Dedicated acoustic forced-alignment models.
- Phoneme-based alignment.
- Other validated alignment methods.

Do not assume that any ASR architecture exposes a valid forced-alignment path.

### 6.1 CTC alignment

If the selected model exposes a valid CTC representation, inspect:

- Blank-token semantics.
- Token sequence mapping.
- Frame stride.
- Receptive field.
- Alignment path construction.
- Repeated token behavior.
- Token boundary interpretation.
- Runtime availability.

Do not treat CTC frame indices as exact phoneme boundaries without accounting for frontend frame shifts and model timing behavior.

### 6.2 Transducer alignment

If the active ASR model is Transducer-based, inspect what alignment information the actual runtime exposes.

Determine whether:

- Token emission timestamps are available.
- Frame-level encoder timing is available.
- Decoder token paths can be inspected.
- Alignment can be reconstructed using a valid algorithm.
- The runtime exposes adequate information.

Do not assume Transducer token emission positions equal actual word start/end boundaries.

Do not fabricate alignment by distributing token times over the audio range.

### 6.3 Dedicated alignment model

If a separate aligner is required:

- Inspect its architecture.
- Inspect its Persian support.
- Inspect its tokenizer or phonetic inventory.
- Inspect its training-data provenance.
- Inspect its artifact format.
- Inspect its license.
- Inspect its runtime requirements.
- Inspect its language limitations.
- Inspect available evaluation evidence.

Do not download large artifacts until metadata, licensing, compatibility, and evaluation eligibility have been reviewed.

### 6.4 Selection decision

Choose the most technically justified method.

Record rejected approaches and reasons.

The decision must account for:

- Persian language compatibility.
- Word-boundary quality.
- Runtime compatibility.
- Memory.
- Latency.
- Offline operation.
- Package size.
- Licensing.
- Maintenance.
- Availability of reliable references.

A method with excellent English results but unverified Persian performance must not be represented as a verified Persian aligner.

## 7. TIMEBASE AND SAMPLE-ACCURATE BOUNDARIES

Create a strict time representation contract.

### 7.1 Primary representation

Where available, use integer sample indices as the primary internal representation.

Each aligned item must preserve:

- Start sample index.
- End sample index.
- Sample rate.
- Source audio identity.
- Timeline origin.
- Time conversion rule.

Define whether intervals are half-open or closed.

Choose one convention and enforce it across the system.

Adjacent words must not accidentally overlap or leave gaps because of inconsistent boundary conventions.

### 7.2 Derived timestamps

Convert sample positions to seconds only through a documented conversion.

Avoid repeated rounding and converting back and forth between floating-point times and sample indices.

Where decimal timestamps are needed for export, retain the original sample indices as canonical evidence whenever possible.

### 7.3 Source offset mapping

Support audio obtained from:

- Full source files.
- Selected ranges.
- Extracted audio.
- Resampled audio.
- Timeline clips.
- Concatenated selected clips, where supported.

Every transformation must have a mapping back to the original source.

If the mapping is not exact, declare its precision and uncertainty.

Do not call a mapping sample-accurate unless the full transformation path preserves exact sample correspondence.

### 7.4 Resampling

If alignment input is resampled:

- Record source rate.
- Record target rate.
- Record input sample count.
- Record output sample count.
- Record resampling implementation.
- Record transformation identity.
- Validate duration preservation.
- Validate sample mapping.

Do not assume a nominal rate ratio is sufficient to reconstruct all source timing behavior.

