# SHAHAB-026 — SPEAKER DIARIZATION, SPEAKER-TURN ATTRIBUTION, OVERLAP DETECTION & PRIVACY-PRESERVING VOICE SEGMENTATION

## 0. MISSION OBJECTIVE

Implement a reliable speaker-diarization subsystem for SHAHAB.

The objective is to determine:

- How many distinguishable speaker turns are present in an audio recording, to the extent supported by the audio.
- Where one speaker's turn ends and another begins.
- Which transcript segments are associated with the same anonymous speaker identity within a recording.
- Where multiple speakers overlap.
- Where speaker attribution is ambiguous.
- Which audio intervals contain speech for which the available evidence is insufficient to identify a stable speaker cluster.

Diarization is not speaker identification.

Diarization attempts to group speech segments by apparent speaker identity within a defined recording or authorized session.

It must not infer the real-world identity of a speaker.

It must not identify a person by name from their voice.

It must not infer sensitive personal traits from voice characteristics.

It must not automatically track a speaker across unrelated recordings.

It must not convert a weak speaker embedding or clustering score into an authoritative identity claim.

The required output is a set of anonymous, recording-scoped speaker references and attributable speech intervals with explicit evidence and uncertainty.

The diarization subsystem must remain separate from ASR, forced alignment, and transcript editing authority.

It must consume their results only through versioned contracts.

## 1. ENTRY GATE

